# No Destructive Commands

## Purpose

Block destructive commands and risky operations unless the user explicitly requests them and the impact is understood.

## When This Applies

- Git operations
- Database migrations
- File deletion
- Deployment changes
- Infrastructure changes
- Package cleanup
- Script execution

## Required Workflow

1. Prefer safe inspection commands first.
2. Explain the risk before any destructive operation.
3. Use dry run, status, diff, validate, or preview modes when available.
4. Back up or preserve user work when possible.
5. Stop when the command could delete data, history, production resources, or user files.

## Standards

- Use reversible operations when possible.
- Do not run destructive database migrations without explicit approval.
- Do not force push protected branches.
- Do not delete broad paths without reviewing contents.
- Do not run unknown scripts from the internet.

## Do Not

- Do not run rm -rf on broad paths.
- Do not use git reset --hard unless explicitly directed.
- Do not use git clean -fdx unless explicitly directed.
- Do not drop, truncate, or reset databases without approval.
- Do not rewrite remote history on protected branches.

## Verification Checklist

- The existing project pattern was inspected before changes were made.
- The implementation is scoped to the user request.
- Unsafe files, secrets, and unrelated changes are excluded.
- Applicable lint, typecheck, test, build, or validation commands were run when available.
- Any skipped or failed verification is reported clearly.

## Related Skills

- Use the matching `.cursor/skills/` workflow when one exists for this rule.
- When no matching skill exists, follow this rule directly and keep changes minimal, safe, and verifiable.
